Mitigating urban light pollution: A review of municipal regulations and implications for planning
Artificial outdoor light at night has greatly contributed to the economic, social, and
cultural life of cities over the last three hundred years and continues to expand worldwide at the
rate of about 2% per year (Kyba et al., 2017). Energy-efficient technologies such as light
emitting diodes (or LEDs) are contributing to higher levels of nighttime illumination, a welcome
development in previously underserved areas, but one that also raises concerns about light
pollution (LP), or the cumulative adverse impact of excess, obtrusive light. Indeed, evidence is
mounting on the risks posed by LP to human health and safety, health of nature and wildlife, and
climate change (Pothukuchi, 2021). Over the last few decades, “dark-sky” advocates have called
for specific policies and practices to mitigate LP.
To what extent, then, are LP concerns addressed by land use and related plans, policies,
and regulations (collectively termed regulations from here on) in U.S. cities, if they are, and how,
are questions taken up in this exploratory study. What rationales, purposes, and adverse impacts
guide regulations and how are conflicts addressed? How do planners and elected officials view
light pollution and what community-level factors support or hinder related decision-making and
implementation? Finally, what recommendations emerge for planning practice and scholarship? I
answer these questions through a mixed-method study that includes a review of municipal plans,
policies and codes such as those related to lighting, recreation, sustainability, energy, and
climate, in eight U.S. cities; a “best-practice” case-study; interviews with local officials and
experts; and a scan of local news outlets for LP related awareness, information, and opinion.
In a first study of its kind, I argue that planners need to pay attention to this topic, have
important roles to play and are well-situated for these roles, and can support a multitude of


3
actions. However, for a variety of reasons, planning lags the knowhow and technology currently
available to mitigate light pollution. Although codification requires a degree of familiarity with
lighting’s multiple impacts and desired parameters, it is not only feasible but also shown to
produce measurable impact in Flagstaff, the model case documented in this paper.
The paper is organized into four major sections. The first briefly traces urban lighting’s
history to showcase its growing importance as a municipal concern, summarizes key impacts of
concern related to LP, and discusses rationales for planning. The following section describes the
research methods used while a third describes and discusses findings. The paper concludes with
recommendations for planning practice and scholarship.
Urban lighting, light pollution, and planning rationales
By 1700, public lighting was established in Amsterdam, Paris, London, and Copenhagen
and elsewhere in Europe, serving mostly elite interests in trade, travel, and socialization
(Koslofsky, 2002, p. 744). The first street lighting consisted of oil lamps in glass-paned lanterns
attached to a post or bracket. London’s early regulations required residents to hang lanterns
outside their houses, and specified size, hours, days of the month (depending on moonlight), and
months of the year (straddling winter), penalties for non-compliance, and exemptions (De Beer,
1941). Oil was replaced by gas in the early 19th century and, towards the end of the century, gas
itself was replaced by electricity (Bouman, 1987). In New York City, Edison developed the first
power company to provide current for lights, which were installed in 1882. By 1887, Los
Angeles had 36 150-ft-high arc-light masts (SurveyLA 2017). Electric incandescent light soon
took off nationwide; demand for light fed the production of power and vice versa (Ploetz, 2002).


4
Municipal governments increasingly adopted responsibility and standards for lighting the entire
city and developed varied forms of financing (De Beer, 1941; Bouman, 1987).
As night time work and leisure expanded, crime-protection grew as an imperative in
particular showplace districts, and business competition and civic boosterism demanded more,
and more spectacular, lighting (Bouman, 1987, pp. 12-14). For example, like other U.S. cities,
Minneapolis had privately provided gas lighting since 1870. After 1892, when the city took over
electric lighting, the central business district, major avenues, and older neighborhoods had more
lamps. Businesses along Hennepin and Nicollet Streets paid for half of the cost of the ornamental
lighting installed privately to promote the businesses. A 1913 article lauded the replacement of
the “ungainly post of iron with its single light” on Nicollet with the “ornamental lamp post of
splendid design with five lights” (Bouman, 1987, pp. 26-28). By the turn of the 19th century,
lighting was expanded to serve property values, civic beautification, recreation and
entertainment, and with the advent of motorized transportation around World War I, even more
road safety. It is worth noting that public lighting’s development disproportionately benefited
elite propertied and business interests (Bouman, 1987).
This brief history points to the growth of urban outdoor lighting in terms of illumination,
geography, rationales and interests, technological innovation, and financing. It also highlights the
central role of local governments in this growth. At the same time, local efforts to regulate light’s
more intrusive qualities also emerged, albeit more sporadically (Ploetz, 2002). Traditionally,
local laws mandate that emitted light does not extend beyond the property line or create
significant glare, codifying such impacts as trespass or nuisance (Ploetz, 2002, p. 1026). This is
keeping with zoning’s broader purpose of allowing property owners to derive benefit from the
property without creating a nuisance for neighbors, and generally securing public health, safety,


5
and welfare. More recently, with greater awareness of light pollution and related sky glow
(discussed below), a few municipalities and states have enacted regulations specifically to
protect astronomical activity, endangered wildlife, and parklands, and to conserve energy
(Ploetz, 2002; City of Flagstaff, AZ, n.d.; City of San Diego, CA, n.d.). Flagstaff’s “best
practice” measures are discussed in a subsequent section.
The earth is getting brighter at night. Kyba et al. (2017) document a 2.2% average annual
increase in artificially lit outdoor area with a total growth of 1.8% in upward radiance per year,
albeit with large differences across regions. Light pollution generally comprises the unwanted,
unintended, and obtrusive aspects of artificial lighting. The International Dark-Sky Association
(IDA) defines it as ‘any adverse effect of artificial light’ (Stone, 2017, p. 285), while
Youyuenyong (2015) calls it “every form of artificial light in the wrong place at the wrong
time.” About 83% of the world’s population and more than 99% of the U.S. and European
populations live under light-polluted skies, defined by Falchi et al. (2016, p. 4)1 as places where
artificial sky brightness at the zenith is greater than 14 mcd/m.2 Stated more plainly, we can only
see about 50 stars without any aids in many metropolitan areas compared with up to 3,000 stars
in dark, rural areas (Mizon, 2012).
A community’s level of local economic development—employment in manufacturing
and retail and wholesale trade, for example—along with its population, income, and density are
correlated with LP, as are its local roadways (Olsen et al., 2014). Many cities are adding new
forms of outdoor illumination, including animated advertising signs and decorative lighting, to
encourage tourism and nightlife (Ngarambe & Kim, 2018; Zielinska-Dabkowska & Xavia,
2019). A recent review concludes that LP has received scant attention by planners and highlights


6
a range of adverse impacts (Pothukuchi, 2021). A few are summarized below. The International
Dark-Sky Association—or IDA—(n.d.-a) offers a wide-ranging compilation of related research.
Select adverse impacts of light pollution
The American Medical Association (2016) has designated LP a public health hazard.
Cupertino et al (2022) summarize research on the adverse human health effects of artificial
indoor and outdoor light. They cite disruptions to sleep and mood, circadian rhythms and
melatonin production; activation of stress hormones and related impacts such as increased risk of
certain cancers and cardio-vascular disease. Additional impacts include loss and degradation of
night-vision (Pottharst and Könecke, 2013). Spectrum, illumination level measured at the eye,
duration of exposure, timing of exposure relative to one’s circadian cycle, and animation, all are
believed to contribute to light’s effects on health, sleep, mood, vigor, and overall wellbeing (see
Schulte-Romer et al., 2019, for example). Higher levels of blue light in LED (Light-Emitting
Diode) lamps exacerbate these disruptions due to LED’s widespread adoption. Municipal
lighting retrofits with LEDs in cities such as Seattle, WA, Davis, CA, and New York, NY, with
higher blue content relative to the previous High Pressure Sodium (HPS) standard, for example,
resulted in widespread resident complaints (AMA, 2016; Chaban, 2015; Scigliano, 2013). More
recently, in accordance with AMA guidance, many cities are using 3000K or lower color
correlated temperature (CCT)2 fixtures, some with dimming measures (DOE 2017, p. 15).
Increasingly, billboards alongside highways and large signs with dynamic lighting are raising
concerns related to driver distraction and glare (Ngarambe and Kim 2018; Zielinska-Dabkowska
and Xavia 2019).
Artificial light at night has wide-ranging impacts on fish, birds, amphibians, reptiles and
mammals, and indeed, entire ecological communities, with especially detrimental consequences


7
for threatened and endangered species (Commonwealth of Australia, 2020; Russart & Nelson
2018; Gaston et al., 2018; and many others). These biodiversity threats apply at multiple scales,
from the neighborhood to national parks and multi-national regions. LP can change behavior
and/or physiology of organisms, thereby reducing survivability or reproductive output. It can
also have the indirect effect of changing the availability of habitat or food resources, and it can
attract predators and invasive pests. In plants, artificial light affects growth, timing of flowering,
and resource allocation, with cascading effects for pollinators and herbivores (Bennie et al.,
2017; Yu et al., 2019). Indeed, some scientists believe LP to be catastrophic to ecosystems to a
degree similar to climate change itself (Berthaume, 2007, p. 57). LEDs pose special threats to
wildlife because many species perceive light beyond the blue range and into the ultraviolet range
(Cabrera-Cruz et al., 2018; Kinzey et al., 2019). To illustrate, a study in Chile documented that
approximately 20,000 Markham’s storm petrel fledglings died hitting lamp posts, disoriented by
the 6600K LED lamps that replaced older ones (Schulte-Römer et al., 2018, p. 51).
Finally, excess light wastes energy and money and exacerbates climate change (Stone,
2017; Li et al., 2019). IDA (n.d.-b) estimates that at least 30% of all outdoor lighting in the U.S.
is wasted, costing $3.3 billion and releasing 21 million tons of carbon dioxide a year. Thus,
simply eliminating wasteful light can contribute to sustainability without affecting intended
service levels. LEDs indeed offer opportunities for LP mitigation because they can be adapted
for better optical control—that is, applying light more efficiently to the target area, dimming
during low-usage times, and manipulating spectral characteristics so as to minimize incidental
negative impacts. However, decisions to adopt LEDs, including for streetlight retrofits in cities,
are almost entirely driven by LEDs’ energy-efficiency and longevity, and thus significant cost
savings (Keatts, 2015). Because of high initial costs, some authorities lock into place long term


8
contracts for LED systems with energy companies or streetlight manufacturers, without a full
consideration of their adverse health and environmental impacts or, indeed, their LP-mitigation
capabilities (Shaw, 2014). Thus, while LED’s exponential growth risks nullifying the energy
gains of the lighting sector, its potential for eco-friendly lighting practice and thus LP mitigation
tends sadly to be under- or un-exploited (Schulte-Romer et al., 2019).
To summarize, adverse impacts of artificial nighttime lighting stem from excess
illumination outside target areas—especially uplight—and at times when lighting may be safely
dimmed or switched off, nuisance glare due to the visibility of the light source causing temporary
blindness or discomfort, and the presence of wavelengths shorter than the orange part of the
spectrum, or about 585 nanometers.
Rationales for planners’ involvement in LP-mitigation
Principal rationales for planning include to prevent and mitigate negative externalities of
economic activities and to support the provision of public goods (Klosterman, 1985). The
previous section’s discussion about the human, ecological, and social impacts of LP, LP’s threats
to humanity’s shared cultural heritage in the view of the natural dark sky, and the need for
collective action for meaningful impact, all thus underscore the need for planners’ attention to
LP. Additionally, LP regulation falls within the ambit of local planning: urban land uses that
contribute to community life and vitality—such as manufacturing, trade, transportation, and
recreation—are also key LP sources (Olsen et al, 2014). Their growth is influenced by local
planning, and zoning codes already contain standards requiring lighting and specifying related
parameters (Ploetz, 2002). Relatedly, municipalities are ideally located to enforce pollution laws
because they have incentive to act, knowledge about sources that present the greatest threat,
flexibility to pursue violators, and the ability to respond quickly (Lehner, 1993). Third, while the


9
costs of wasted light and related carbon impacts indeed are informing policies, they do so in
incomplete ways and with contradictory results. This sets the stage for public and stakeholder
engagement in decisions in which planners routinely facilitate information-sharing across
boundaries, deliberation about goals and strategies, negotiation, and consensus-building (Innes
and Booher, 2010; Forester, 2006; Burby, 2003). Significant knowledge about LP’s adverse
impacts as well as desired lighting qualities for LP mitigation is already available, as is related
technology, all of which should ease related planning collaborations with professionals in public
lighting, utilities, design, and environmental conservation. Local planners are thus well situated
to help shape related policies. Interest in the topic appears to be growing: the American Planning
Association’s Planning Advisory Service received more than 400 lighting-related inquiries in the
previous decade, according to a 2004 Zoning Review article (Crawford, 2004, p. 3).
A final rationale relates to planners’ ethical obligations in light of LP’s ecological and
disparate social impacts. The benefits and costs of excessive artificial light are distributed
unevenly with many of the benefits—from advertising, extension of shopping hours, nighttime
entertainment, etc.—going to business owners, while many costs, such as adverse health and
ecological impacts and the loss of the night sky, are borne by the public as a whole and nature
and wildlife. Planners are exhorted to pay special attention to the needs and interests of less
powerful groups, future generations, and non-human actors, and to preserve the natural
environment (APA-AICP, 2016). As urbanization spreads across the globe, planners are urged to
conserve nature and wildlife in the urban communities in which they work (Beatley, 2011).
Exploring light pollution regulation in eight cities: Methods


10
How do localities regulate LP, if they do so at all? What rationales, purposes, goals, and
adverse impacts among those discussed above (or others) guide policies? What is the content of
policies, and how do they vary based on land use or purpose, such as street lighting, sports
arenas, or signs? How do they balance competing objectives such as safety, advertising, energy
efficiency, and biodiversity? What, if any, are exemplary approaches to LP mitigation? How do
planning and public lighting professionals and elected officials view LP and community-level
factors that might support or hinder related policy-making and implementation? Finally, what
recommendations emerge for planning practice and scholarship?
To answer these questions, I launched in 2020 a qualitative mixed-method study in which
specific questions and methods emerged incrementally as the study unfolded. As an initial step, I
reviewed zoning codes, comprehensive plans, and, if available on city websites, other policies
such as those related to environment, streets, and design—in eight cities in the United States. I
selected two of the largest cities—one northern and one southern—from each of the four major
ecological zones dividing the continental U.S. from east to west. See Table 1. I was interested in
larger cities distributed across the country, ones that likely would have the full range of land uses
implicated in excess lighting, as well as municipal capacity to develop and enforce LP-related
policies. The ecological zones delineate key bird-migration corridors or flyways, thereby
offering an ecological parameter for case-selection (IDA, n.d.-c). Birds exist within everyday
urban landscapes and migratory birds are dramatically affected by LP. Northern and southern
cities represent divergent seasonal characteristics and temperature ranges that have implications
for outdoor activity at night. I was also interested in exploring the state of LP-related policies in
“regular” cities, that is, those that lack the compelling rationales that typically drive local LP
control, such as the presence of major astronomical observatories, national parks, or the


11
protection of endangered species. The selected list includes some of the country’s largest cities,
all with universities nearby as well as resources in environmental and technological fields.
