Zoning and other codes are of special interest in the study because they contain standards
that are legally enforceable. To isolate terms pertaining directly or indirectly to LP, a research
assistant and I searched for keywords such as light, light pollution, illumination, luminaire, glare,
sky glow, light trespass, uplight, spill-light, lumens, LED, and others derived from a review of
policy-oriented research on light pollution first in zoning codes, then expanding to other codes
such as those related to utilities, signs, and streets. Finding only minimal evidence of LP-related
concerns, we searched on city websites for master plans, neighborhood plans, and other plans
and reports. Within relevant sections, we independently coded explicit and implicit rationales for
requiring, encouraging, or restricting lighting, and relevant standards such as lighting provision
and operation, minimum and maximum illumination levels, curfew for dimming or switch-offs,
location, fixture and light characteristics, and other pertinent features. I also analyzed the lighting
code of Flagstaff, Arizona, widely considered best-practice with documented mitigation impact.
After assembling LP-related findings (or lack thereof), I reached out by email and
telephone to planners in each study city to obtain reactions to findings, contextual information
and perspective on specific LP measures or their absence, and possible motivators for change in
the community. I interviewed a total of 12 individuals—local planners, lighting agency staff, a
local expert, and an elected official—from six out of the eight sample cities and Flagstaff, with
non-response, despite several attempts, from New York and Nashville. Interviews ranged from
about 15 minutes to nearly an hour. Finally, we scoured news media sources in, or pertinent to,
each city to gauge the nature of LP-related concerns, actions, and controversies, if any existed,


12
over the last decade. Content derived from interviews and media reports was coded for responses
to the study’s questions and also to identify relevant unanticipated or emerging themes. Note that
the main objective of this exploratory study was to sketch the state of practice related to LP
control and mitigation in some of the country’s largest cities rather than to investigate lighting
standards per se or to compare cities across specific categories.
Findings and discussion
Lighting provision and regulation in municipal codes
Expectedly, zoning codes of study cities commonly reference such longstanding
rationales for outdoor lighting as promoting public safety, safe and comfortable navigation for
pedestrians and other travelers, business and entertainment, sports and recreation, and aesthetic
character. See Table 2. Accordingly, standards require the provision of light in various districts,
corridors, and activity areas, and specify minimum illumination levels, duration and
directionality (to minimize light trespass into neighboring, especially residential, properties).
They specify fixture characteristics such as pole heights and spacing intervals; require that
lighting must not conflict with federal aviation rules or be confused for traffic signals; and may
detail luminaire elements such as light source, shielding, and lens-type. Less typically, they may
specify light characteristics such as coordinated-color temperature (CCT) and color-rendering
index (CRI) 3 Additionally, codes require lighting schemes to be compatible with the existing
pattern and function of the area or to preserve historic or aesthetic character. Expectedly, more
permissive standards for illumination occur in industrial, commercial, entertainment districts;
some even have special sign districts.
