13
In addition to such light-affirming rationales and standards, all codes also regulate to
prevent or minimize glare, nuisance trespass affecting adjacent properties—especially
residences—and public streets, and distraction to drivers posed by dynamically illuminated
billboards. Standards to prevent glare and trespass typically call for shielded luminaires, though
seldom with specificity on desired performance. For example, Houston requires “shades or hoods
on fixtures” to prevent light from shining into residential properties and Jacksonville calls for the
design and operation of lighting systems to minimize glare, shadow, light pollution and light
trespass. An exception is New York’s requirement that lights in public areas abutting residential
uses have semi-cut-off shielding such that no more than 5% of peak illumination radiates above
90 degrees and no more than 20% above 80 degrees of nadir (the lowest point of the lamp).
A couple codes also specify a 10 PM curfew for playfields (Nashville and Seattle) with
Los Angeles additionally requiring automatic timer control with on-off switching for recreation
areas and prohibiting the use of floodlights at construction sites after 6 PM. Most codes also
specify maximum levels of trespass illumination, such as 0.5 foot candle, crossing into residential
areas or 1 foot candle into non-residential areas (Nashville); or 0.01 foot candle at 15 feet from
the boundary of the target property (Los Angeles). A few places give property owners
(Jacksonville, FL) or municipal officials (Los Angeles) discretion to lower lighting levels from
those specified in the zoning code.
Seattle’s code is unique among study cities to cite a preference for zero lighting in
waterfront areas: “Artificial night lighting shall first be avoided.” If that is infeasible, the code
urges “minimal impacts on the aquatic environment,” including with the use of shades and by
focusing light on the pier surface. Certain waterfront recreation areas are required to be without
artificial illumination altogether. More typically, codes urge restraint in waterfront areas and


14
scenic corridors in general terms. Los Angeles stipulates street lighting on scenic corridors to be
kept to “minimum useful intensity” and located only at intersections and parking sites and away
from the roadway. Without detail, the city’s code also instructs that emission of artificial light
from any seaside industrial/ commercial facility be controlled. Thus, codes’ curbs on adverse
impacts of lighting on the natural environment are general exhortations rather than specific
performance standards. On the other hand, the specific measures identifying trespass, such as the
0.5 or 1.0 foot candle noted above, are without scientific basis, according to a lighting expert I
interviewed.4
Sign-related lighting codes
As the billboard industry increasingly incorporates LED technologies with dynamic
effects, color changes, and videos, concerns related to glare and trespass onto residential
properties and distraction to roadway users are heightened. Standards acknowledge commercial
interests while also seeking to minimize adverse impacts on residential properties, users of
streets and outdoor spaces, and the aesthetic character of the area. Sign-related codes typically
specify particular eligible districts or corridors where illuminated signs may be installed;
minimum distance of such signs from residences, streets, and intersections; directionality,
character, and operation of lighting; limits on dimensions of illuminated portion; and less often,
curfews related to extinguishing or dimming of lighting.
To illustrate, codes in Jacksonville, Nashville and Omaha prohibit sign lighting from
causing glare or otherwise impairing vision. Omaha’s specifies a maximum illumination level of
5200 nits5 during daylight hours or 0.3 foot candle higher than ambient level, whichever is less;
and between dusk and dawn, a maximum level of 500 nits or 0.3 foot candle higher than ambient
level, whichever is less. Houston allows a generous maximum of 1250 nits between dusk and


15
dawn for certain dynamic signs, and 3500 nits for Special Facility Display Signs. Study cities
typically disallow signs with flashing lights in all or most districts, making exceptions only for
specific areas such as convention, entertainment, and arena districts.
The above discussion showcases the affirmative nature of outdoor lighting standards: to
facilitate night-time travel and outdoor comfort, business, entertainment, and other activities
while also promoting public safety and protecting residences from nuisance glare and light
trespass. Rationales related to broader public health, environmental conservation, LP-control,
and dark-sky concerns are typically absent or written as preferences rather than standards. The
vast majority lack standards specifying fully-shielded luminaires (allowing zero light above the
horizontal plane), light sources, or spectral qualities of illumination. No code limits the
cumulative density of lighting in any land use district, activity area, or property, and none seeks
justification for new or additional lighting.
LP mitigation in other policies, local interviews, and media reports
Beyond zoning and other relevant ordinances, content analysis of comprehensive, area,
and sectoral plans (such as for environment or transportation) and policies revealed a mix of
results (See Table 3). Documents from most cities were silent on LP issues or repeated the glare
and light trespass concerns of zoning codes discussed previously. Policies from Chicago, Los
Angeles, and Seattle contained mention of light pollution, including the need to direct light away
from wetlands (Seattle) and protect birds (Chicago). The Framework Element of Los Angeles’
Comprehensive Plan identifies a goal to “protect and preserve the nighttime environment ... and
otherwise minimize or prevent light pollution...” (Goal 9P). We also found reference to ‘sky
glow’ and a goal related to “a street-lighting system that protects and preserves the nighttime


16
environment ... with minimum impact on the environment ...” in the Granada Hills-Knollwood
Community Plan in Los Angeles (CF 15).
Omaha’s Master Plan has the greatest coverage of light pollution issues of this study in
its Environment element. For example, the Visual Resources section (p.22) states that lighting
should “minimize adverse impacts of ... light pollution on ... night-sky views,” with
recommendations to establish standards that minimize impacts on wildlife, encourage reduction
in the operation of lights that may adversely affect wildlife (p.22), and collaborate with
professionals in evaluating impacts on wildlife, particularly migrating birds (p.27). The Building
Sites section recommends the development of a “‘dark sky’ lighting ordinance that directs light
away from the sky” and possible “increased limits on the amount of light that is allowed to cross
property lines” (p.85).
In response to Chicago’s 2006 Nature and Wildlife Plan containing a goal to reduce
“negative effects of Chicago’s ambient light on nocturnal animals and insects,” an Update (2011
2016) reports that a public-private collaboration expanded the “Lights Out!” program to protect
migrating birds from crashing into illuminated buildings, whereas “no specific programs have
been formulated to address other animals and insects.” (pp. 17, 30). The Chicago River Design
Guidelines Checklist contains elements related to lighting including color temperature of LED
bulbs (white light with color temperature of 3000K or below) and light pollution (“dark sky
compliant” fixtures) (pp 73, 80). Lamenting the merely advisory status of the guidelines, the
Chicago planner added, “how we treat lighting doesn’t do enough” for birds and other wildlife.
