Interviews with other key informants similarly confirmed and contextualized findings
from the code and policy review. Lighting-affirmative rationales such as pedestrian and traffic


17
safety, navigation comfort, and effective functioning of various districts and activities were
reinforced in interviews. Planners communicated the tension between public perception that
more light is better and the imperative to support divergent objectives including safety and
aesthetics. Emphasizing pedestrian and bike safety especially during winter, for example, as well
as the associated risk of liability, our Seattle planner discussed the city’s decision not to dim
LED streetlights later at night, despite the availability of such adaptive controls. On the other
hand, complaints from drivers are driving calls for stricter regulation of illuminated billboards, as
interviews of planners in Los Angeles and Omaha revealed.
Interviews also highlighted specific restrictions that incidentally or unintentionally reduce
light pollution. For example, Jacksonville’s Whitehouse naval training airfield requires strict
optical controls on nearby outdoor lights so as to simulate mid-ocean night landings on ships, a
finding confirmed in the city’s 2030 Comprehensive Plan. In another example, California’s
Proposition 218 requires cities to conduct ballot initiatives asking residents to vote to assess
themselves for the costs of streetlight maintenance. Lighting districts in which a majority of
residents reject the assessment have their lights turned off, effectively creating darkness in those
districts. Our LA respondent also pointed to the absence of specific LP-related activism or
grassroots clamor to control LP, and thus of political champions, as a key reason for lack of
intentional LP control, a common explanation for the lack of attention to LP across cities.
Albeit less frequently, respondents described technical challenges to implement LP
mitigation. For example, our Seattle respondent discussed efforts to change luminaires following
community complaints related to the earlier 4000K LED installations. While they are voluntarily
changing luminaires to 3000K or less, such fixtures fall short of some SDOT requirements for
arterials for illumination levels, uniformity, and efficiency given current pole spacing. Because


18
streetlights are funded from the city’s general fund, and given LED’s longevity, upgrading is
done only as current lights fail. Spending moratoriums on streetlight conversions during the
Covid pandemic have created additional delays.
In diverse cities, LED streetlight conversions dominate news media reports related to
outdoor lighting over the last decade. Such reports credit energy efficiency as the driver with
claims of environmental—meaning CO2 reduction—as well as budgetary benefits. Gavin (2021)
reports that the Chicago Smart Lighting Program’s conversion of 270,000 HPS lamps to LED
translates into 50% of energy-savings over 10 years, equivalent to approximately $100 million.
The article also cites an industry survey finding that 185 of the 300-plus largest US cities are
pursuing LED street-lighting, representing a market that ranges from 600,000 to 1.5 million units
per year and a 35% projected penetration of smart streetlights in the country by 2030. Even
though energy and cost savings arguments pervade media reports, concerns also exist related to
the disruptive effects of excess lighting resulting from such conversions in Chicago (Fruend
2020; Stark 2018) and Houston (Holeywell 2017; Shelton 2016); negative effects of the blue
heavy LED lighting including on migrating birds in New York City (Gibberd 2006; Norman
2019); and on amateur astronomy in Nashville (Hughes 2018, Gonzalez 2011) and, as well,
concerns related to pedestrian deaths due to inadequate street lighting in Nashville (Gonzalez
2021, Miller 2022).
Despite LED’s promise of cost-savings due to energy efficiency and longer life-span,
initial set up costs are high. Increasingly, energy service companies (ESCOs) are funding
conversion projects which help cities avoid such up-front costs and, at least on paper, realize
significant savings over a number of years (Gavin 2021). Jacksonville’s streetlight conversion
program, however, raised concerns about even the cost-savings argument. To illustrate,


19
Stepzinski (2016) reported that the city claimed savings of at least $1 million annually in
electricity costs after full replacement. However, data obtained from the city in 2020 showed that
the program ended up costing nearly $35 million, a 45% increase over the 2014 projection of $24
million (Becker, 2021). Furthermore, the LED lights that replaced the high-pressure sodium ones
have a color temperature of 4000K, much higher than the 3000K maximum recommended by the
American Medical Association for streetlights.
Enacting LP control can be politically challenging. Responding to opposition from the
business community, Governor Pataki of New York refused to sign a bill that would require local
government action to advance dark sky objectives (McKinley 2002). For more than a decade
afterwards, New York city councilmembers repeatedly introduced amendments to the city’s
codes, without success, to require that streetlights and other external lights be fully shielded and
have a CCT of no more than 3000K (See Table 3). This analysis thus reveals a pressing
conundrum for advocates of LP control: even as advocates themselves center, entirely
legitimately, energy-efficiency and costs-savings in their case for regulating LP, LED
conversions and higher lighting levels are, nevertheless, resulting in increasing LP, along with
their varied human and ecological impacts. At the same time, powerful stakeholders challenge
local LP control policies, as seen in New York City. Thus, even increasing awareness of the need
for urgent climate action has yet to yield the nuanced understanding of potential conflicts in
objectives related to carbon mitigation and biodiversity protection in LED conversions or,
equally significantly, of the possibilities for achieving both (and other values) in the specification
of LED lamps and related adaptive controls. And where both, knowledge and willingness to
change, exist, technical hurdles, financial constraints, or the fear of liability can delay or stymie


20
desired actions such as selecting lamps that are safer for wildlife or dimming or turning off lights
later at night.
What specific actions might planners propose, then, to obtain meaningful impact, at the
very least, in reducing the rate of LP growth in cities, if not achieving more ambitious
objectives? Planners might study cities that have adopted specific LP-related standards and
compliance protocols and that track impacts. Planners might also peruse “model ordinances”
developed over the last couple decades by the International Dark Sky Association (IDA, n.d.-d;
IDA-IES 2011; IDA 2018; Crawford, 2004). Several papers offer surveys of and or planning
advice on local lighting codes (Green et al, 2022; Luginbuhl et al, 2009; Ploetz, 2002; Crawford,
2004; Jewkes 1998); one study, however, documents positive impact of the codes (Luginbuhl et
al, 2009, discussed in the next section). It is beyond the scope of this paper to offer a detailed
discussion of IDA’s model ordinances. Briefly, however, IDA-IES’s Model Ordinance (2018)
has been criticized by several authors, including for permitting more light in many districts
resulting in sky glow impacts that are greater than what can be expected even when lighting is
unregulated (Illinois Coalition for Responsible Outdoor Lighting, 2011; Morgan-Taylor, 2015;
Luginbuhl, 2013). IDA’s (2018) Certified Dark Sky Community (CDSC) model ordinance has
been adopted by 17 U.S. communities (IDA, n.d.-d). Among other standards, the ordinance
requires full shielding of lamps over 1000 lumens, and limits short-wavelength light and the total
density of illumination, all helpful guidance for planners. The CDSC certification, however,
involves self-identified communities—typically smaller towns in mostly non-urbanized
regions—that display “exceptional commitment to and success in” pursuing dark sky
preservation and put into place several measures to ensure continuing certification (IDA, 2018).
Flagstaff, Arizona’s Lighting Code


21
Few studies document the impact of lighting codes. Luginbuhl et al (2009) is a notable
exception for their investigation of Flagstaff, Arizona, whose standards are widely considered to
be a model for achieving dark sky objectives, even if they are far from perfect. The study finds a
17% growth in light escaping into the sky from 1989 to 2003, compared to an expected 43%
increase had the city’s lighting code that limits total outdoor lighting on new construction not
been enacted. Additionally, if all legally nonconforming lighting installed before 1989 were
brought into compliance with the code, sky glow in Flagstaff could decrease by 36% compared
to that in 2003; moreover, if all lighting, including residential, could be converted to fully
shielded fixtures, sky glow would decrease to about half the current value.
Analyzing both the specific (per acre or per unit) and total amounts of light used for
different land uses, the study documents sports lighting as the largest user of lighting or 32% of
the city’s total when it is on (pp. 197-198). Apart from sports lighting, the most intense users are
commercial establishments—such as gas stations, restaurants, shopping centers, and hotels
accounting for 36% of total output when sports lighting is all turned on and 53% when it is off.
Roadway and residential lighting account for 12% and 14% respectively, with the remaining
21% arising from industrial and institutional uses and automobiles (p. 198). Significantly, unlike
most other cities, Flagstaff’s roadway lighting is nearly universally fully-shielded, contributing
to its relatively lower portion of the total output. Thus, data obtained on lighting installed before
and after the adoption of the lighting code helps convey both how Flagstaff might have looked
like today without the code as well as possible gains if all lighting were brought into
conformance with present standards and if all outdoor lighting were perfectly shielded to avoid
diffusion outside of target areas.


22
Briefly, the lighting element of Flagstaff’s Zoning Code contains three principal objective
standards to limit the cumulative adverse impacts of lighting. See Table 4 for additional details.
Table 4 about here.
a) Limits on the total amount of light used per acre. Lumens per acre caps produced 65%
plus reduction in uplight output for new developments relative to the level expected if the caps
had not been adopted in 1989 (Luginbuhl et al, 2009, p. 201). The 2022 amended lighting code
identifies two lighting overlay zones, Lighting Zones (LZs) 1 and 2, specified with reference to
proximity to the US Naval Observatory, with nearer areas permitted less output per acre. Each
zone has specific standards related to maximum output, shielding, and curfew for different land
uses within each zone.
For example, the code allows a maximum of 17,500 lumens per acre for commercial,
industrial, and multi-family uses in LZ1, and 35,000 lumens per acre for these uses in LZ2. For
single family or duplex residential lots, the code allows a maximum of 1,000 lumens per lot or
parcel in LZ1 and 5,000 lumens per lot or parcel in LZ2. Because fixtures attached to
buildings—especially on walls with low reflectance qualities—and mounted under roof
overhangs or canopies contribute less ambient light than free-standing ones distant from
buildings, less output from the latter types is allowed in LZ1.
Notably, these lumen counts are lower than the previous version’s 25,000 and 50,000
lumens per acre for LZs 1 and 2, respectively, for industrial, commercial, and multi-family uses,
and 10,000 lumens per parcel for single family and duplex lots in both zones. Flagstaff-based
respondents contextualize these changes as a response to the increasing use of more efficient
LED technology and the phasing out of LPS lights for which the previous standards incorporated
a 0.7 multiplier to measure actual output. The updated code also reduces lighting zones from


23
three to only two. These changes were among six key recommendations from a study of the
impact of current and projected lighting trends on the continued operation of the Naval
Observatory (Coconino County, 2019).
b) Full- or near-full shielding of luminaires. The city’s lighting code defines three classes
of lighting for residential and non-residential outdoor areas: Class 1, where color rendition is
important, such as for outdoor eating, sales areas such as gas station canopies, and primary
entry/exit areas; Class 2, such as general roadway and parking lighting; and Class 3, or
decorative lighting such as that used for illuminating buildings, flags and landmarks, or
landscape elements. For each class, the code specifies zones where fully-shielded lighting is
required, where it is preferred even if partially-shielded lighting is permitted, and where lamps
above a certain lumen output are prohibited altogether.
The code requires fully-shielded fixtures for Class 1 and 2 lighting in commercial,
industrial, and multi-family uses for LZs 1 and 2. For these uses, Class 3 lamps with an output
below 1,750 lumens are required to be fully shielded in LZ1 while they may be partly shielded in
LZ2; lamps at and over 1,750 lumens are prohibited in both zones. For single-family and duplex
residential lots, lamps of all Classes with an output of less than 700 lumens are required to be
fully shielded in LZ1 and permitted to be partially shielded in LZ2; lamps at and over that count
are prohibited in both zones. As with the lumens per acre standard, these standards too represent
a reduction of allowable lumen-output per fixture from the previous version to adjust for LED’s
greater lighting efficiency.
c) Lamp standards. Additionally, the 2022 Lighting Code requires all Class 1 lighting to
have a CCT of 2700K or less, and Narrow-Spectrum Amber LED lighting for all Class 2 lighting
applications. The updated standards also restrict the spectral range of the light source relative to


24
the older Code, specifying peak wavelength to be between 589 and 595 nanometers. (The
previous allowable range was 585 to 595 nanometers.) All light sources may be used for Class 3
lighting, including those incorporating color changes. While all LED lights contain blue
wavelengths, narrow-band Amber LED lighting reduces the content of blue and higher-energy
wavelengths while also offering energy, astronomical, and visual sky glow advantages.
As for signs, except for exposed neon lighting, the Code also treats external illumination
for signs as Class 1 lighting, counts it toward the total outdoor light output limits, and requires
that external lighting be steady, stationary, downward directed, and fully shielded. The Code also
distinguishes standards for displays of letters and symbols on signs in Lighting Zones 1 and 2
and imposes a time limitation of 9 PM in LZ1, and 11 PM or when the business closes,
whichever is later, in LZ2.
Thus, Flagstaff’s Lighting Code offers objective standards organized by land use, land
area, Lighting Zone designation, and fixture type. As before, all lighting owned and operated by
public agencies is required to be fully shielded. Applicable to new construction and substantial
rehabilitation or replacement or change in use, the standards nevertheless stand in stark contrast
to those related to the cities reviewed in the first section. Thus, standards operationalize the
Code’s purpose as outlined in its opening: to “help assure that dark skies remain a resource to be
enjoyed by the Flagstaff community and its visitors, minimize light pollution and light trespass
across property lines, maintain an environment that the City’s astronomical industry depends
upon, reduce energy consumption, minimize the potential disruption to nocturnal ecosystems,
and to allow safe and efficient outdoor lighting.”
Flagstaff’s planner explained the lighting code’s development and its recent updates. As
LPS sources were phased out in the marketplace and LED usage became ubiquitous, the city saw


25
wholesale replacements using LED fixtures especially on commercial properties, often without
the requisite permits. Following a Joint Land Use Study (Coconino County, 2019) conducted in
partnership with the county, nearby military installations and other stakeholders, to assess the
impact of existing and projected illumination, standards were modified to apply the stricter LZ2
standards to LZ 3, revise total lumen output per acre and per fixture, and restrict further the
allowable spectrum for Narrow-Spectrum Amber LEDs. “We work with property owners and
contractors all the time. For commercial owners, there’s a lot of hand-holding ... For example, in
an old mall, we worked to bring them in compliance by showing how they could phase their
replacements,” our contact noted.
In keeping with its status as the world’s first Certified Dark-Sky Community, the city
dedicates funds to support enforcement, education, and outreach to encourage voluntary
compliance and track non-complying fixtures and properties, and bolstered these with the
purchase of a spectrometer and related technology. The lighting enforcement office is also
responsible for assembling annual Dark Sky reports posted to the city’s website. Professional
astronomers affiliated with local observatories offered a great deal of expertise over the years in
the development of the code, trained staff and elected officials, developed educational materials,
participated in media and community outreach, and advised studies. “It took a lot of work to
become the world’s first certified first Dark Sky Community,” our planner noted, however, “it is
not as hard as it is sometimes made out to be. It is possible. It is feasible. We’re the example.”
Offering other levers for communities without significant astronomical activity, such as
arguments related to LED’s impacts on circadian rhythms and nature, she stressed the need to
make informed tradeoffs to balance multiple values: “White LED is more efficient than narrow
band Amber LED, but a little more electricity is worth the lower impact on dark sky and


26
nocturnal wildlife.” Flagstaff thus owes its success to efforts over more than sixty years to
control sky glow, with ongoing technical assistance from astronomers, outreach to residents and
policy makers, and support from state and federal government, all in the face of the rapid
embrace of more light-polluting sources and cheaper technology, as well as community growth.
This study has a few limitations. A first planning study of its kind, it examined zoning
codes in a relatively small sample of cities for LP regulation. As such, it was designed to offer a
bird’s eye view of the state of practice, not to compare cities’ codes or performance with each
other. Beyond zoning and other ordinances, it investigated communities’ goals and policies
related to LP only for those cities for which such documents were readily available. A more
thorough search might reveal greater desire and perhaps even intention to regulate LP than is
evident from the documents reviewed. Finally, it is possible that additional political or technical
challenges (or opportunities) might surface in more in depth case studies of individual cities with
wider participation of representatives. These and other gaps could be filled in future scholarship.
Conclusion
Zoning and other ordinances in some of the major cities across the country address
outdoor artificial lighting predominantly in terms of its historic role in promoting night time
navigation, safety, advertising, and recreation, with restrictions typically designed to avoid
nuisance caused by glare and spillover beyond property boundaries. However, even such
restrictions are plagued by lack of definition, merely encouraging language, or arbitrary
measures. It is also unclear to what extent standards are enforced beyond permit review. A small
smattering of master plans, area and sectoral plans, and agency reports are starting to


27
acknowledge LP impacts on the environment, wildlife, and dark sky, albeit inconsistently across
policies in the same city and without enforceable standards.
Still, existing codes that already require shielding of lamps, directionality of light,
maximum output per fixture, and curfews for full switch-off or dimming, might be reviewed
against community values and goals, specified more precisely and consistently and with regard
to newer technologies, and combined for cumulative impact, all without eroding functional
requirements related to safety and comfort. By requiring fully-shielded outdoor fixtures
especially for streets, parking areas, and billboards, for example; luminaires with CCT of 2700K
or less; and 9 PM or 11 PM curfews depending on land use, it is possible to limit increases in
light pollution through zoning codes. Conversions of street lights and other public lighting offer
a starting point for implementation and related public outreach and engagement.
Beyond sharpening existing standards, cities might explore how lesser-known LP
controls may be phased in, such as limits on: a) total illumination output per acre for different
land uses; b) output per luminaire based on purpose, shielding, attachment to and distance from
buildings, and other parameters; and c) wavelengths of light in the blue-violet range. Technology
exists to adopt the above parameters but modifying wholesale the existing layout of light poles
and fixtures to achieve desired qualities of illumination nevertheless will involve costs. Thus,
upgrades might be made to Narrow-Spectrum Amber (NSA) LED sources for general purpose
lighting, as existing lamps fail. A more ‘middle-range’ recommendation for streets and parking is
Phosphor-Converted Amber LED lamps which are twice as efficient as the NSA LED ones and
have more blue and green wavelengths than are desired for dark sky impact, but are superior to
the more commonly installed White LED lamps.


28
Given the somewhat technical nature of these recommendations, planners will need to
educate themselves about the growing impacts of light pollution including due to the widespread
adoption of LEDs, and specific features of outdoor lighting that can contribute to as well as
potentially mitigate LP. They might engage elected officials, stakeholders, and residents in
deliberations to clarify values and goals with respect to lighting and translate these into desired
standards and benchmarks for implementation, drawing knowledge as well as inspiration from
cities like Flagstaff. They might review plans and policies related to land use, environment and
natural resources, transportation, design, and others, to ensure consistent treatment affecting
outdoor lighting. They will also need to help leaders grapple with the tradeoffs associated with
cost-savings versus optimally combining objectives in human health, biodiversity, and carbon
mitigation, and in negotiating an uneven arena consisting of lighting promoters such as
manufacturers, trade associations, and businesses on the one hand, and naturalists, astronomers,
and other LP-control advocates on the other.
University courses in city planning and landscape and architectural design might
incorporate LP’s human, social, and environmental impacts and strategies for mitigating LP in
existing settlements and minimizing it in new developments. Such material might be appended to
syllabi related to environmental and natural resource conservation, biophilic urban design, urban
infrastructure (including related finance), sustainability (broadly defined, that is, going beyond
energy efficiency), and cultural values and assets. As NYC’s experience shows, LP mitigation
encompasses the conflicts and tradeoffs illustrated in Campbell’s (2016) sustainability triangle,
which also might merit exploration in the classroom.
As cities are called to address climate shocks, biodiversity loss, and social wellbeing and
equity, they undoubtedly will benefit from the creation of federal standards related to light


29
pollution such as those currently present for air and noise pollution. Leading professional
organizations—such as International Dark-Sky Association, American Medical Association, and
perhaps even the American Planning Association—might also offer unambiguous, ongoing
guidance to help communities achieve desired impacts through zoning and building codes in
ways that incorporate emerging technology. In combatting LP, it will be important for advocates
and planners to communicate early and often the ways in which more light is far from benign in
its impacts; that “environmentally-sensitive” means more than “energy-efficient”; and that
tackling light pollution is possible without sacrificing safety or functionality of outdoor spaces.
